<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  THE LOOP KIND FOR `scxml-core-engine` — the decisions `ai_loop.scxml` must
  not make on that repository's behalf, written by that repository.

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND IT IS NOT "because debt did not fit".

  Four runs of `scxml-core-engine` work were driven under `unclaimed` on
  2026-09-11 (runs 315, 317, 320, 324). Every one of them ended
  `exhausted (duration)` with the work unfinished, and the product said the
  same sentence each time: *THE SAME WORK, with more room.* The hour was not a
  judgement about the work; it was the daemon's constant arriving because
  `unclaimed_loop.scxml` authors nothing and `plugins.rs`'s `duration()` falls
  to its `None` arm.

  That is the file working exactly as designed. `unclaimed` is the honest
  answer for a tree that has written no decisions, and its own header says what
  a tree that wants decisions does instead: *writes their OWN document beside
  this one and names that kind*. This is that document.

  ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE OWNER'S INSTRUCTION THAT REACHES IT was already recorded, in
  `plugins.rs` beside the three-armed `duration()` — *every sprag loop
  unbounded* — with the note that folding `Never` into the absent case is what
  made that instruction "sayable only by a caller overriding the bound every
  round, which is register item 853's forbidden shape". A `max_seconds` named
  on each launch is that forbidden shape. A document is the shape that was
  built for it.

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ WHAT DECLINING THE THREE GUARDRAILS COSTS, STATED BEFORE THE CLAUSE
  THAT DECLINES THEM.

  `DEFAULT_MAX_SECONDS`'s own doc says what the hour catches that nothing else
  can: *a run whose steps have stopped making progress but have not stopped,
  which counts no iterations and spends nothing while it holds a pane.* It is a
  liveness check wearing a clock's clothes, not a work budget.

  So declining it removes the only liveness check this build has by default —
  because `ai_loop.scxml` ships `stall_after_steps` as `'never'`, which is off.
  The pair is not optional and is not a matter of taste: `kind.rs` asserts it,
  and `debt_loop.scxml` says why in its own words — *it must not become
  `never` … this is now the ONLY ceiling of the five this kind admits that
  measures progress at all.*

  ⇒ Every number below that says `never` is paid for by `stall_after_steps`
  being a number, and by `progress_marks` naming things that actually move.
  Reading one without the other reads this document wrong.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="decided"
       name="sce_loop">

  <datamodel>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH DIALOGS A RUN OF THIS KIND MAY ANSWER *YES* TO: NONE.

      Same reasoning as `unclaimed_loop.scxml`, and it does not weaken because
      this kind has an author. A clause here is a standing yes for every future
      run in this tree, authorising questions nobody has read. The tree's own
      `CLAUDE.md` draws the same line for pushes and for destructive acts; a
      kind document is not the place to widen it. A caller who needs one names
      it on their own run.

      ⚠ The runs this kind was written for already work without one: 315, 317,
      320 and 324 were launched with no `may_answer` and none of them ended
      `blocked`.
    -->
    <data id="may_answer" expr="[]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT A RUN OF THIS KIND IS REDIRECTED TO MID-FLIGHT: NOTHING.

      A screen rule refuses a dialog and types an instruction in its place. Its
      first key is a refusal, so it can grant nothing — but it still decides on
      a future author's behalf, in this author's language. Left empty for the
      same reason `may_answer` is.
    -->
    <data id="screen_rules" expr="[]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH BLOCKED TURNS GET A SECOND AGENT'S OPINION: NONE.

      A paid model call per blocked turn, standing, for every run in this tree.
      A run that wants one asks for it.
    -->
    <data id="judged_rules" expr="[]"/>

    <!--
      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ HOW MANY TURNS A RUN OF THIS KIND MAY TAKE: NO CEILING.

      `'never'` is the document's idiom for *do not bound this*, and it is the
      same word `guardrails` below uses — deliberately, because inventing a
      second spelling would make two identical meanings look different.

      WHY, and it is a measurement rather than a preference: a round in this
      tree is dominated by BUILDS, not by thinking. Measured 2026-09-11 on this
      machine, in this repository: the Kotlin forge-runtime jar is ~11 s warm,
      `scripts/gate forge-cpp` 12 s, `forge-go` 33 s, a full
      the whole `sce-build` suite under the cli feature **1052 s**, and a push runs the
      path-selected gate set for minutes. A turn ceiling denominated in turns
      therefore buys nothing a stall bound does not buy better: a run doing one
      long build is indistinguishable, by turn count, from a run doing nothing.

      ⚠ What ends a run of this kind is `stall_after_steps` — nothing moved —
      not an arithmetic limit on how much work it was allowed to do.
    -->
    <data id="max_turns" expr="'never'"/>

    <!--
      ⛔⛔⛔ HOW OFTEN THIS RUN REPLACES ITS OWN SESSION: EVERY 8 TURNS.

      A reflection writes what was learned, closes the agent's session and opens
      a fresh one that reads it. That is what lets one run outlive one context —
      and it is not free: the new session re-pays the fixed prefix, and in THIS
      tree the prefix is large. `CLAUDE.md` plus the project's own instructions
      are loaded every session, and the documents a round must consult are big
      (`SCE_MESH.md` is 4,170 lines, `SCE_FORGE.md` 1,852,
      `docs/SCE_ACCEPTED_SUBSET.md` carries the whole diagnostic appendix).

      ⚠⚠ 8 RATHER THAN `debt_loop.scxml`'s 5, and the difference is derived, not
      preferred. Measured across the four runs this kind was written for: 7, 7,
      21 and 4+ iterations inside one hour — so a round here costs between three
      and nine minutes of wall clock. At 5 the slow end reflects roughly every
      45 minutes, which is fine; but runs 315 and 317 banked TWO atomics in
      seven iterations, so a reflection at 5 would have cut a round in half and
      paid a cold start to resume work the same session already had in hand.
      8 sits above the observed atomic size and still below the context ceiling.

      ⚠ RE-DERIVE THIS rather than trusting it. The population is four runs, and
      four runs is not a population — `debt_loop.scxml` says exactly that about
      its own refuted first attempt. The command:
        sprag runs -t <session>   # iterations per run, and what each banked
    -->
    <data id="reflect_every" expr="8"/>

    <!--
      HOW MANY TIMES IN A ROW A CHECK MAY REFUSE THE AGENT'S CLAIM before the
      run stops buying another turn and reflects instead: 2.

      Following `debt_loop.scxml` here, and saying so rather than implying a
      derivation this tree has not got: no run of this kind has yet been
      refused by a `milestone_check` at all, so there is nothing local to
      measure. 2 is the value a shared daemon has evidence for.

      ⚠ The template's 3 was not chosen for this tree either; 2 is preferred to
      it because a refusal here costs a turn that may contain a 17-minute test
      run, so buying a third is expensive in a way it is not elsewhere.
    -->
    <data id="reflect_after_refusals" expr="2"/>

    <!--
      HOW MANY TIMES A RUN MAY RE-AIM ITSELF: ONCE.

      Same as `debt_loop.scxml`, for a reason this tree shares: a run that
      re-aims twice has stopped doing the work it was given and started
      choosing work, and the choosing belongs to whoever wrote the brief.

      ⚠ Measured relevance: all four runs of this kind re-aimed zero times —
      each was given one atomic and worked it. This clause has not yet bitten,
      which is the state to keep it in.
    -->
    <data id="reaim_max" expr="1"/>

    <!--
      HOW MANY TIMES ONE QUESTION MAY BE PUT AGAIN: 2. Following the shared
      value; no local measurement exists, and stating that is the point.
    -->
    <data id="reask_max" expr="2"/>

    <!--
      🎯🎯🎯 WHO SAYS A MILESTONE WAS REACHED — the generic checker.

      `claude -p` with JSON output, which is what `debt_loop.scxml` uses and
      what the owner asked for on 2026-09-02, quoted in that file: *전부
      공통되게 해야해, 지금 프로젝트말고 다른 프로젝트에서도 자동으로 적용되게
      범용적으로 들어가야해*.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THIS CORRECTS A CLAIM THE SKILL STILL MAKES. The debt-repayment-loop
      skill says a kind names a checker that *reads ONE tree's own record*, and
      that a run of another tree judged by it is "being told *no* by a document
      that has never heard of it". That was true when the checker was a
      repository script. It is not true of `claude -p`, which is handed the
      run's own brief and the tree it stands in. The half that remains
      tree-specific is the PROSE below (`working_rules`, `reference`) — which is
      the actual reason this document exists.
    -->
    <data id="milestone_check" expr="'claude -p --output-format json'"/>

    <!--
      WHO SAYS A PROPOSED NEXT MILESTONE IS ONE TO TAKE: the same generic
      checker, for the same reason.
    -->
    <data id="successor_check" expr="'claude -p --output-format json'"/>

    <!--
      WHAT A SERVICE OUTAGE LOOKS LIKE ON THE SCREEN, and how long to wait it
      out. Following the shared values: these describe the AGENT's provider, not
      this tree, so a per-tree number here would be a number about somebody
      else's service.
    -->
    <data id="service_needles"
          expr="[
            'service is temporarily unavailable',
            'upstream connect error',
            'Overloaded'
          ]"/>
    <data id="service_retry_ms" expr="600000"/>
    <data id="service_retry_text"
          expr="'The provider was unavailable. Continue the milestone from where it stopped.'"/>

    <!--
      ⛔⛔ HOW MUCH THIS SESSION MAY HAVE READ before the next milestone is taken
      in a fresh one: 800,000 tokens.

      A CAPACITY bound, not a cost knob — splitting one task across sessions is
      MORE expensive, because a cache write costs about twenty times a cache
      read and a fresh session re-pays the fixed prefix. So the number belongs
      near the end of the window, not in the middle of it.

      ⚠ Same as `debt_loop.scxml`, and here the sharing is justified rather than
      copied: the constraint is the MODEL's context window, which is a property
      of the agent and not of the repository. What differs per tree is how fast
      the window fills, and that is `reflect_every`'s business above.
    -->
    <data id="context_ceiling" expr="800000"/>

    <!--
      WHAT A RUN OF THIS KIND DOES AS IT CLOSES.

      ⚠ Naming the tree's own contract rather than a generic tidy-up: a round
      here is not finished when the code is written. `CLAUDE.md` and the memory
      index both say the judge is CI, and that the push's verdict is read from a
      file rather than from a wrapper — a background job's completion
      notification reported "exit code 0" twice on 2026-08-16 while
      `origin/main` had not moved.
    -->
    <data id="closing_rules"
          expr="'Before reporting done: (1) the working tree is clean or every \
remaining change is named and explained; (2) any push was judged from its own \
rc file and confirmed with `git ls-remote origin main`, never from a wrapper \
exit code; (3) what was NOT finished is stated plainly, with the measurement \
that says so.'"/>

    <!--
      WHAT TO DO WITH A QUESTION NOTHING AUTHORISED: stop and say so. No
      standing answer exists (`may_answer` is empty), so the honest end is a
      blocked run a person can read, not a guess.
    -->
    <data id="unanswered_rule"
          expr="'Do not guess at a dialog nothing authorised. Stop, and let the \
run report what was asked and which options were offered.'"/>

    <!--
      WHAT TO DO WITH A PANE THAT CANNOT BE READ, and with an agent that is
      unwell. Shared values: both describe the terminal and the provider, not
      this tree.
    -->
    <data id="unreadable_rule"
          expr="'If the pane cannot be read, stop rather than typing into a \
screen whose state is unknown.'"/>
    <data id="unwell_rule"
          expr="'If the agent reports it cannot continue, stop and say so \
rather than re-prompting.'"/>

    <!--
      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ THE STANDING RULES A RUN OF THIS KIND IS GIVEN.

      ⚠⚠⚠ These are PROSE, and prose is not enforced: no `cond` reads this, no
      gate holds a run to it. `debt_loop.scxml` says the same about its own, and
      that is why the things that MUST hold are gates in the tree rather than
      sentences here. What belongs here is what a fresh session could not
      reconstruct from the tree in one turn.
    -->
    <data id="working_rules"
          expr="'1. Builds and tests go through ~/.claude/remote-build/bin/bx. It \
decides where to run and keeps the full log; a bare cargo/ctest invocation on \
this host competes with other sessions for a machine that is usually in swap.\n\
2. The judge is CI, not a local batch. Verify locally only the target you \
directly changed; commit, push, and read the previous run per-job at the start \
of the next round. Do not block a round waiting for a hosted run.\n\
3. Judge a push from its OWN rc file and confirm with `git ls-remote origin \
main`. A background job notification reports the compound command status and \
has said &quot;exit code 0&quot; while origin had not moved.\n\
4. Before committing, run tools/git-hooks/gate_registry.py with the \
changed-from and ci-owed flags. Without a changed argument it prints usage \
and exits 0, which reads as a pass.\n\
5. Commits follow COMMIT_FORMAT.md: &lt;type&gt;: &lt;subject&gt; at 72 chars, 1-3 \
contiguous bullets each at 72 chars, no emoji, no Co-Authored-By.\n\
6. ONE WRITER PER TREE. A supervisor may be reading this tree. Never `git add \
-A`; stage by path, and read `git diff --cached` before committing. Leave work \
you did not write uncommitted.\n\
7. A measurement beats a document, including this one and including CLAUDE.md. \
Where the two disagree, re-derive from the tree and say that the document is \
stale.\n\
8. An absent signal is not a passing one. A skipped check, an empty grep and a \
golden that was regenerated all look like success; prefer a check that fails \
when the thing it guards is missing.'"/>

    <!--
      WHERE A RUN OF THIS KIND STARTS READING.

      ⚠ Entry points, not a reading list: each of these is a door onto more, and
      naming more here would make the prompt longer without making the first
      turn better.
    -->
    <data id="reference"
          expr="'/home/coin/scxml-core-engine/CLAUDE.md — the project contract, \
and the file to distrust first when the tree disagrees.\n\
/home/coin/scxml-core-engine/ARCHITECTURE.md — read before touching either \
engine.\n\
/home/coin/scxml-core-engine/COMMIT_FORMAT.md — read before the first commit.\n\
/home/coin/scxml-core-engine/SCE_ERROR_CONTRACT.md and \
docs/SCE_ACCEPTED_SUBSET.md — read together before touching diagnostics.\n\
/home/coin/scxml-core-engine/claudedocs/ — the working RFCs; gitignored, so \
treat them as notes rather than as contract.\n\
/home/coin/.claude/projects/-home-coin-scxml-core-engine/memory/MEMORY.md — \
the index of what previous rounds measured. Its ▶ NEXT section is the entry \
point; the files it links carry the evidence.'"/>

    <!--
      WHEN THE PANE IS READY TO BE TYPED INTO: when the agent has settled.
    -->
    <data id="ready_when" expr="{ match: 'settles', marker: 'claude' }"/>

    <!--
      🎯🎯🎯🎯🎯 THE THREE BOUNDS THIS KIND DECLINES — and the clause two below
      is what pays for it. Read them together or not at all.

      The owner's instruction, recorded in `plugins.rs`: *every sprag loop
      unbounded*. The measurement that brings it here: four runs of this tree's
      work, every one ended by the daemon's hour with the work unfinished and
      the product itself saying THE SAME WORK, with more room.

      ⚠⚠ AND THE HOUR WAS NEVER ABOUT THE WORK. `DEFAULT_MAX_SECONDS` catches
      "a run whose steps have stopped making progress but have not stopped".
      Declining it is only legitimate because `stall_after_steps` below measures
      exactly that, directly, instead of inferring it from a clock.
    -->
    <data id="guardrails"
          expr="{
            max_bytes:      'never',
            max_iterations: 'never',
            max_seconds:    'never'
          }"/>

    <!--
      HOW LONG A PERSON MAY HOLD A RUN OF THIS KIND: SIX HOURS.

      A held run types nothing and spends nothing, so it cannot be bounded by
      any of the three above; without this it parks forever. Six hours is an
      afternoon of reading a pane, which is the shape of holding this is for —
      not a night. Same as `debt_loop.scxml`, and the reasoning is about people
      rather than about either repository.
    -->
    <data id="hold_within_ms" expr="21600000"/>

    <!--
      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ THE ONLY CEILING THIS KIND KEEPS, AND THEREFORE THE ONLY THING
      THAT MEASURES PROGRESS AT ALL. 150 STEPS.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT MUST NOT BECOME `never`. `kind.rs` asserts the pair — a kind that
      declines its three guardrails must keep this a number — and the assertion
      is the reason the decline above is allowed to exist. The tempting move the
      day it fires on something inconvenient is to spell `never` here; that
      would leave this tree's runs with no progress measure of any kind.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT IT IS NOT: not a clock and not a spend. A run that builds for an
      afternoon and commits is never touched by it. What it ends is a run that
      is still typing while nothing below has moved.

      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE NUMBER IS PROVISIONAL, WHICH IS SAID HERE RATHER THAN
      DISCOVERED LATER. `debt_loop.scxml` derived its 1300 from 2,050 commits
      and every run log the daemon holds, and it records that its own first
      attempt — derived from FOUR runs — was refuted by the full population. The
      population available to this kind today is also four runs (315, 317, 320,
      324), so the same objection applies to this number and it is not being
      hidden.

      What those four support: the per-run step counts are 9, 10 and 24 by the
      driver logs, against 7, 7 and 21 iterations — so a step here is close to
      one agent turn, and 150 is roughly six to twenty hours of turns in which
      nothing was committed and nothing was pushed. That is far above any
      legitimate stretch observed (every one of the four banked a commit inside
      its hour) and far below "never".

      ⚠ RE-DERIVE IT once this kind has run enough to have a population:
        for f in /run/user/1000/loop/run*.log; do printf '%s %s\n' \
          "$(wc -l < "$f")" "$f"; done | sort -rn | head
      and take the longest stretch that ended in a commit, not the longest run.
    -->
    <data id="stall_after_steps" expr="150"/>

    <!--
      ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ WHAT A ROUND OF THIS KIND MOVES WHEN IT IS GETTING SOMEWHERE —
      the other half of the bound above, and the half that keeps its predicate
      from being one thing.

      ⚠⚠⚠ A LIST AND NOT ONE SIGNAL, for the reason `ai_loop.scxml` records: the
      obvious predicate is *the agent has not said it reached a milestone*, and
      it is wrong in the direction that matters — a run doing one large piece of
      work properly reaches no checkpoint for a long time, so a one-signal bound
      catches the runs doing the best work first.

      ⚠⚠ WHY THESE TWO, for this tree specifically. `.git/logs/HEAD` moves on
      every commit, which is what a round here banks. The second is what
      `CLAUDE.md` makes this repository's real unit of done: a round is not
      finished at the commit but at the PUSH, because the judge is CI. Measured
      2026-09-11: runs 315, 317 and 320 each moved both, and each moved the
      second within the same hour it moved the first.

      ⚠ Neither is a file this repository maintains by hand, which is
      deliberate — a progress mark somebody has to remember to update is a
      progress mark that stops being one.
    -->
    <data id="progress_marks"
          expr="[
            '.git/logs/HEAD',
            '.git/refs/remotes/origin/main'
          ]"/>

    <!--
      WHICH TREE A RUN OF THIS KIND MAY BE OPENED IN: one that is a git
      checkout. The predicate, not a path — the same document compiles into
      every checkout, so a literal path here would be a decision about one
      machine.
    -->
    <data id="works_in" expr="'.git'"/>

    <!--
      WHOSE SCREEN IT MAY APPEAR ON: the window named for the tree.
    -->
    <data id="stands_in" expr="'tree'"/>

    <!--
      WHICH DIMENSION A RUN OF THIS KIND MUST KEEP WHOLE: rows.

      ⚠ Height is the dimension this tree's rounds actually need. A reflection's
      follow-up read fails on a short pane, and the supervisor skill records
      that a `kill-pane` on a sibling has left panes at 36 rows where 73 was
      asked for. Width costs a wrapped log; height costs a lost reflection.
    -->
    <data id="keeps" expr="'rows'"/>
  </datamodel>

  <!--
    Nothing happens here. The datamodel above IS this document, and the driver
    reads it without ever stepping the machine.
  -->
  <final id="decided"/>
</scxml>
